# Line-ending policy for this repo (added 2026-07-27, direct instruction).
#
# The problem this fixes: with no .gitattributes and `core.autocrlf=false`
# (Git for Windows' own system default here), git neither converts on
# commit nor on checkout -- it stores whatever bytes the last tool to
# write a file happened to produce. The tools used on this project
# disagree: a Windows editor (TeXstudio) and anything writing through
# Python's default text mode (`Path.write_text()` on Windows) emit CRLF,
# while other tooling emits LF. So slides/ had drifted to a 533 CRLF /
# 130 LF mix, and simply opening four HFT slides in an editor and saving
# them produced a 200-line, content-free diff (2026-07-27, batch #15).
#
# The fix: `text eol=crlf` -- git normalizes to LF *in the repository* on
# commit (so it no longer matters which convention the writing tool used:
# same content = no diff, ever), and checks the file back out with CRLF
# in the working directory, which is what Windows editors and .bat files
# want. Cost: one one-time renormalization commit rewriting the stored
# bytes of every file that was previously stored with CRLF.
#
# Deliberately an explicit per-extension list rather than the usual
# blanket `* text=auto`: `text=auto` decides text-vs-binary by looking for
# a NUL byte in a file's first 8000 bytes, and this repo contains real
# binaries that pass that test -- beameruserguide.pdf,
# gotham-safetybox.pdf and three media/*.eps charts have no NUL that
# early, so a blanket rule could silently EOL-mangle (i.e. corrupt) them.
# Anything not listed below keeps today's behaviour of being left
# completely alone, and the binary declarations at the bottom make that
# guarantee explicit for the formats where it actually matters.

# --- Text: stored LF, checked out CRLF ---------------------------------
*.tex        text eol=crlf
*.bib        text eol=crlf
*.sty        text eol=crlf
*.dtx        text eol=crlf
*.ins        text eol=crlf
*.latexmkrc  text eol=crlf
*.md         text eol=crlf
*.txt        text eol=crlf
*.py         text eol=crlf
*.csv        text eol=crlf
*.svg        text eol=crlf
*.R          text eol=crlf
*.Rd         text eol=crlf
# .bat in particular *must* be CRLF in the working tree; this guarantees it
*.bat        text eol=crlf
# The two R-package text files that carry no extension this list catches;
# both are tool-generated (roxygen2, RStudio) and so are exactly the kind
# of file that flips convention when the tool is upgraded or swapped.
*.Rproj      text eol=crlf
NAMESPACE    text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never touched, never sniffed ------------------------------
*.pdf        binary
*.png        binary
*.jpg        binary
*.jpeg       binary
*.eps        binary
*.xlsx       binary
*.pptx       binary
*.docx       binary
*.lnk        binary
*.synctex.gz binary
